IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `clwa-dates`

-- Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT), chapters 2, 3, 4, 7, 7A, 8, 8A and 14 -- liability, defences and damages: the
-- questions of the person bringing a claim for personal injury or death.
--
-- SEAT: the claimant: the injured person; after a death, a member of the dead person's family or the estate; for mental
-- harm, the relative who suffered it; for child abuse, the person abused as a child. "Can I sue the volunteer who hurt
-- me?", "What can the estate recover?", "Am I a member of the family?", "Can I get damages for my psychiatric injury?",
-- "Is contributory negligence presumed against me?", "How much of my earnings counts?", "How much are my damages cut?",
-- "Is the organisation taken to have breached its duty?", "What is the most my lawyer can charge?". The defendant, the
-- insurer, the organisation and the court get no seat: what the court decides is asked as a fact.
--
-- Every question takes plain facts. No question takes a DEONTIC or an EVENT. The one duty with a deadline in these
-- chapters that a claimant meets (an unincorporated body's 120 days to nominate a defendant, s 114D (3), s 114E (1) (a))
-- is a regulative rule run inside a question.
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED: ss 5, 8, 9 (good samaritans, volunteers); s 14 (apologies); ss 15, 16 (survival); ss 23, 24-27
-- (death); ss 32, 34-36 (mental harm); ss 37, 38 (terrorism and motor vehicles); s 43 (precautions); ss 92-98 (pt 7.1);
-- s 100 (domestic services); s 102 with ss 27, 47, 168 (3) (contributory negligence); ss 107B-107C (apportionable
-- claims); s 113 (roads); ss 114A-114BE, 114D, 114E (child abuse); s 181 (costs). The rest is out: NOTES.md.
--
-- Edition: Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT), A2002-40, Republication No 77 (R77), effective 16 May 2026, deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/A2002-40.txt. The Civil Law (Wrongs) Regulation 2003 is not deposited: where it may
-- prescribe a number (s 181 relevant amount and percentage) the Act's own number is used.
--
-- (c) Australian Capital Territory. Used under CC BY 4.0 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md). Unofficial text.
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. Judgments the Act hands to a court are INPUTS. Answers follow the rulings
-- on negatives of 2026-11-04 and 2026-11-05 (NOTES.md): "(the text's no)" and "NOTHING-kind" mark the two kinds of no.

§ `Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002: liability, defences and damages -- the questions of the person bringing a claim`

§§ `Part 2.1 and part 2.2 -- Good samaritans and volunteers`

-- s 5 (1): a good samaritan does not incur personal civil liability for an act or omission done honestly and without
-- recklessness in assisting (or advising about) a person apparently injured, at risk of injury, or needing emergency
-- medical assistance. (2): not if the liability falls within a compulsory third-party motor vehicle scheme, or the good
-- samaritan's capacity was significantly impaired by a recreational drug. (3): despite (2) (b), the protection applies
-- to administering naloxone honestly and without recklessness to a person apparently overdosing on an opioid.
-- s 5 (4): good samaritan "means" a person acting without expectation of payment who comes to the aid of such a person
-- (or a medically qualified person giving advice by telephone or other telecommunication).
DECLARE `Is the good samaritan protected` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the good samaritan incurs no personal civil liability for this (s 5 (1))`
    `yes: naloxone given honestly and without recklessness for an opioid overdose is protected even if they were impaired by a recreational drug (s 5 (3))`
    `no: the protection does not apply where the liability falls within a compulsory third-party motor vehicle insurance scheme (the text's no, s 5 (2) (a))`
    `no: the protection does not apply where their capacity was significantly impaired by a recreational drug (the text's no, s 5 (2) (b))`
    `no: s 5 protects only acts done honestly and without recklessness, by a good samaritan as s 5 (4) defines one (the text's no)`

@export Can I hold the person who came to help me personally liable, or are they protected as a good samaritan (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) s 5)? A good samaritan who helps without expecting payment, honestly and without recklessness, incurs no personal civil liability, except within a CTP motor scheme or when impaired by a recreational drug.
GIVEN `no expectation of payment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who helped you acted without expecting payment or other consideration
      `came to my aid or advised by phone` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they came to your aid, or (as a doctor, health professional or paramedic) advised by telephone or other telecommunication about your treatment, when you were apparently injured, at risk of injury or needing emergency medical help
      `honestly and without recklessness` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether what they did (or failed to do) was done honestly and without recklessness
      `within a CTP scheme` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the liability falls within a compulsory third-party motor vehicle insurance scheme (for example, the Motor Accident Injuries Act 2019 scheme)
      `impaired by a recreational drug` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether their capacity to exercise appropriate care and skill was significantly impaired by a recreational drug at the time
      `naloxone for an opioid overdose` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether what they did was administering naloxone to you because you were apparently overdosing on an opioid, to resuscitate you
GIVETH A `Is the good samaritan protected`
`s 5 -- is the good samaritan protected from personal liability` `no expectation of payment` `came to my aid or advised by phone` `honestly and without recklessness` `within a CTP scheme` `impaired by a recreational drug` `naloxone for an opioid overdose` MEANS
    IF NOT (`no expectation of payment` AND `came to my aid or advised by phone` AND `honestly and without recklessness`)
    THEN `no: s 5 protects only acts done honestly and without recklessness, by a good samaritan as s 5 (4) defines one (the text's no)`
    ELSE IF `within a CTP scheme`
         THEN `no: the protection does not apply where the liability falls within a compulsory third-party motor vehicle insurance scheme (the text's no, s 5 (2) (a))`
         ELSE IF NOT `impaired by a recreational drug`
              THEN `yes: the good samaritan incurs no personal civil liability for this (s 5 (1))`
              ELSE IF `naloxone for an opioid overdose`
                   THEN `yes: naloxone given honestly and without recklessness for an opioid overdose is protected even if they were impaired by a recreational drug (s 5 (3))`
                   ELSE `no: the protection does not apply where their capacity was significantly impaired by a recreational drug (the text's no, s 5 (2) (b))`

-- s 8 (1): a volunteer does not incur personal civil liability for an act or omission done honestly and without
-- recklessness while doing community work for a community organisation on a voluntary basis; (2): not within a CTP
-- scheme, for defamation, when significantly impaired by a recreational drug, or acting (knowingly or when they ought to
-- have known) outside the authorised activities or contrary to instructions. s 9 (1): the liability attaches instead to
-- the community organisation. s 9 (2): the injured person may sue the volunteer personally only if it is clear that the
-- s 8 protection does not apply, or the organisation disputes in a defence filed to the action that it is liable.
-- "Community work" (s 7) and "community organisation" (s 6) are asked as facts (fork F9).
DECLARE `Whom can I sue for what the volunteer did` IS ONE OF
    `the community organisation: the volunteer's liability attaches to it, and you may not sue the volunteer personally (s 8 (1), s 9 (1), (2))`
    `the community organisation (s 9 (1)), and also the volunteer personally, because the organisation disputed in its filed defence that it is liable (s 9 (2) (b))`
    `the volunteer personally: it is clear the s 8 protection does not apply (s 8 (2), s 9 (2) (a))`
    `the volunteer personally: s 8 protects only acts done honestly and without recklessness, in community work for a community organisation on a voluntary basis (the text's no)`

@export Whom can I sue for an injury a volunteer caused me: the volunteer or the community organisation (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) ss 8, 9)? A volunteer doing community work honestly and without recklessness is protected and the organisation is liable instead, unless an exception applies.
GIVEN `community work for a community organisation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the volunteer was doing community work (for example religious, educational, charitable, sporting, environmental or community purposes) for a corporation that coordinates volunteers' community work
      `voluntary basis` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they were unpaid for the work (or paid within limits set by regulation)
      `honestly and without recklessness` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether what they did (or failed to do) was done honestly and without recklessness
      `within a CTP scheme` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the liability falls within a compulsory third-party motor vehicle insurance scheme
      `defamation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is for defamation
      `impaired by a recreational drug` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether their capacity to do the work properly was significantly impaired by a recreational drug at the time
      `outside authority or against instructions` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they were acting, and knew or ought to have known they were acting, outside the activities the organisation authorised or contrary to its instructions
      `organisation disputes liability in its defence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have sued the organisation and it disputes, in a defence filed to the action, that it is liable for what the volunteer did
GIVETH A `Whom can I sue for what the volunteer did`
`s 8, s 9 -- whom can I sue for what the volunteer did` `community work for a community organisation` `voluntary basis` `honestly and without recklessness` `within a CTP scheme` `defamation` `impaired by a recreational drug` `outside authority or against instructions` `organisation disputes liability in its defence` MEANS
    IF NOT (`community work for a community organisation` AND `voluntary basis` AND `honestly and without recklessness`)
    THEN `the volunteer personally: s 8 protects only acts done honestly and without recklessness, in community work for a community organisation on a voluntary basis (the text's no)`
    ELSE IF `within a CTP scheme` OR `defamation` OR `impaired by a recreational drug` OR `outside authority or against instructions`
         THEN `the volunteer personally: it is clear the s 8 protection does not apply (s 8 (2), s 9 (2) (a))`
         ELSE IF `organisation disputes liability in its defence`
              THEN `the community organisation (s 9 (1)), and also the volunteer personally, because the organisation disputed in its filed defence that it is liable (s 9 (2) (b))`
              ELSE `the community organisation: the volunteer's liability attaches to it, and you may not sue the volunteer personally (s 8 (1), s 9 (1), (2))`

§§ `Part 2.3 -- Apologies`

-- s 12: part 2.3 applies to civil liability of any kind, but not to damages or compensation for defamation or under the
-- Discrimination Act 1991 or the Workers Compensation Act 1951. s 14: an apology is not an admission of fault or
-- liability, is not relevant to deciding fault or liability, and evidence of it is not admissible in a civil proceeding
-- as evidence of fault or liability. A flat negative: the text's no.
DECLARE `What the apology counts for` IS ONE OF
    `nothing as to fault: the apology is not an admission of fault or liability, is not relevant to deciding them, and cannot be used in evidence of them (s 14)`
    `part 2.3 does not apply to defamation, Discrimination Act 1991 or Workers Compensation Act 1951 claims (the text's no, s 12 (2)); this Act says nothing more about the apology (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Does an apology from the person I blame count as an admission that they are at fault (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) ss 12-14)? No: an expression of sympathy or regret is not an admission of fault or liability and is not admissible as evidence of it, except in defamation, discrimination and workers compensation claims.
GIVEN `defamation, discrimination or workers compensation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is for damages or compensation for defamation, or under the Discrimination Act 1991 (ACT) or the Workers Compensation Act 1951 (ACT)
GIVETH A `What the apology counts for`
`s 14 -- does the apology count as an admission` `defamation, discrimination or workers compensation` MEANS
    IF `defamation, discrimination or workers compensation`
    THEN `part 2.3 does not apply to defamation, Discrimination Act 1991 or Workers Compensation Act 1951 claims (the text's no, s 12 (2)); this Act says nothing more about the apology (NOTHING-kind)`
    ELSE `nothing as to fault: the apology is not an admission of fault or liability, is not relevant to deciding them, and cannot be used in evidence of them (s 14)`

§§ `Part 2.4 -- Survival of actions on death`

-- s 15: on death, causes of action vested in the person survive for the benefit of the estate (not defamation).
-- s 16: exemplary damages are not recoverable (2). If the act or omission caused the death, the damages are worked out
-- without regard to any loss or gain to the estate from the death, and do not include pain and suffering, bodily or
-- mental harm, loss of expectation of life (3) (b) (i), loss of earning capacity or future earnings (3) (b) (ii), or
-- s 100 domestic services damages (3) (c); but (4) the (3) (b) (i) damages may be recovered for an asbestos-related
-- disease if the person gave notice under s 51 before dying and died of it (or it contributed). (5)-(7): reasonable
-- funeral expenses paid or payable by the estate may be included, reducible for age and health.
DECLARE `A head of damages for the estate` IS ONE OF
    `exemplary damages`
    `pain and suffering, bodily or mental harm, or loss of expectation of life`
    `loss of capacity to earn, or loss of future probable earnings`
    `loss of capacity to perform domestic services (s 100)`
    `reasonable funeral expenses paid or payable by the estate`
    `other damages (for example medical expenses or earnings lost before death)`

DECLARE `Can the estate recover it` IS ONE OF
    `yes: it may be recovered by the estate (s 15, s 16)`
    `yes: reasonable funeral expenses may be included, though the court may reduce them having regard to the dead person's age and health (s 16 (5), (6))`
    `yes: for an asbestos-related disease, notified under s 51 before death, that caused or contributed to the death (s 16 (4))`
    `no: exemplary damages are not recoverable in a surviving cause of action (the text's no, s 16 (2))`
    `no: where the wrong caused the death, these damages are not included (the text's no, s 16 (3))`
    `s 16 (5) allows funeral expenses only where the wrong caused the death; otherwise it says nothing (NOTHING-kind)`
    `no: causes of action for defamation do not survive under part 2.4 (the text's no, s 15 (2))`

@export Can the dead person's estate recover this kind of damages on the claim the person had when they died (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) ss 15, 16)? Where the wrong caused the death, the estate cannot recover pain and suffering, loss of expectation of life or future earnings (with an exception for asbestos-related disease), and never exemplary damages.
GIVEN `the head of damages` IS A `A head of damages for the estate` @desc Which kind of damages the estate is claiming
      `defamation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the dead person's cause of action was for defamation
      `the wrong caused the death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the act or omission that gave rise to the claim caused the person's death
      `asbestos-related disease` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the claim was for personal injury from an asbestos-related disease (asbestosis, asbestos induced carcinoma, asbestos-related pleural diseases, mesothelioma, or a disease prescribed by regulation)
      `notice given before death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person gave notice of claim under s 51 before they died
      `the disease caused or contributed to the death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person died of the asbestos-related disease, or it contributed to the death
GIVETH A `Can the estate recover it`
`s 16 -- can the estate recover this` `the head of damages` `defamation` `the wrong caused the death` `asbestos-related disease` `notice given before death` `the disease caused or contributed to the death` MEANS
    IF `defamation`
    THEN `no: causes of action for defamation do not survive under part 2.4 (the text's no, s 15 (2))`
    ELSE CONSIDER `the head of damages`
         WHEN `exemplary damages` THEN `no: exemplary damages are not recoverable in a surviving cause of action (the text's no, s 16 (2))`
         WHEN `pain and suffering, bodily or mental harm, or loss of expectation of life` THEN
            IF NOT `the wrong caused the death`
            THEN `yes: it may be recovered by the estate (s 15, s 16)`
            ELSE IF `asbestos-related disease` AND `notice given before death` AND `the disease caused or contributed to the death`
                 THEN `yes: for an asbestos-related disease, notified under s 51 before death, that caused or contributed to the death (s 16 (4))`
                 ELSE `no: where the wrong caused the death, these damages are not included (the text's no, s 16 (3))`
         WHEN `loss of capacity to earn, or loss of future probable earnings` THEN
            IF `the wrong caused the death`
            THEN `no: where the wrong caused the death, these damages are not included (the text's no, s 16 (3))`
            ELSE `yes: it may be recovered by the estate (s 15, s 16)`
         WHEN `loss of capacity to perform domestic services (s 100)` THEN
            IF `the wrong caused the death`
            THEN `no: where the wrong caused the death, these damages are not included (the text's no, s 16 (3))`
            ELSE `yes: it may be recovered by the estate (s 15, s 16)`
         WHEN `reasonable funeral expenses paid or payable by the estate` THEN
            IF `the wrong caused the death`
            THEN `yes: reasonable funeral expenses may be included, though the court may reduce them having regard to the dead person's age and health (s 16 (5), (6))`
            ELSE `s 16 (5) allows funeral expenses only where the wrong caused the death; otherwise it says nothing (NOTHING-kind)`
         WHEN `other damages (for example medical expenses or earnings lost before death)` THEN `yes: it may be recovered by the estate (s 15, s 16)`

§§ `Part 3.1 -- Wrongful act or omission causing death`

-- s 23: "member, of a dead person's family--each of the following people is a member": (a) a domestic partner; (b) a
-- child (including a child born alive after the death); (c) a person to whom the dead person acted in place of a parent;
-- (d) a parent; (e) a person who acted in place of a parent to the dead person; (f) a brother, sister, half-brother or
-- half-sister; (g) a former spouse, civil union partner or civil partner. "child includes a grandchild and a stepchild";
-- "parent includes a stepfather, a stepmother, a grandfather and a grandmother". Fork F10: the list is read as closed, so
-- anyone else is the text's no.
DECLARE `My relationship to the dead person` IS ONE OF
    `their domestic partner`
    `their child, grandchild or stepchild (including one born alive after the death)`
    `someone they were acting in place of a parent to, immediately before the death`
    `their parent, step-parent or grandparent`
    `someone who was acting in place of a parent to them, immediately before the death`
    `their brother, sister, half-brother or half-sister`
    `their former spouse, civil union partner or civil partner`
    `someone else (for example an aunt, uncle, cousin, niece, nephew, step-sibling or friend)`

@export Am I a member of the dead person's family under the Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) s 23? The Act's list covers a domestic partner, children (including grandchildren and stepchildren), parents (including step-parents and grandparents), those in place of a parent either way, brothers and sisters (including half), and former spouses or partners.
GIVEN `my relationship` IS A `My relationship to the dead person` @desc How you were related to the person who died
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 23 -- am I a member of the dead person's family` `my relationship` IF
    NOT (`my relationship` EQUALS `someone else (for example an aunt, uncle, cousin, niece, nephew, step-sibling or friend)`)

-- s 26: in assessing damages for a death, the following must not be taken into account to reduce them: insurance paid on
-- the death or injury; superannuation, provident fund, friendly society, lodge or union benefits; payment instead of
-- leave, a pension or another social security or veterans benefit paid because of the death or injury; a gratuity on
-- the death; a house or household contents a family member acquires after the death; life insurance premiums that would
-- have become payable. A flat "must not": the text's no to a reduction. Anything else: s 26 is silent (NOTHING-kind).
DECLARE `A payment I received because of the death` IS ONE OF
    `money paid under a contract of insurance on the death or injury`
    `money from a superannuation, provident or similar fund, or a friendly society, benefit society, lodge or trade union`
    `a payment instead of leave, or a pension or other social security or veterans benefit, paid because of the death or injury`
    `a gratuity paid on the death`
    `a house, or household contents, acquired by a family member after the death, used as their home`
    `life insurance premiums that would have become payable had they lived`
    `something else (for example an inheritance from the estate, or workers compensation)`

DECLARE `Does it reduce my damages for the death` IS ONE OF
    `no: it must not be taken into account to reduce damages for the death (the text's no, s 26)`
    `s 26 does not list it, and this Act does not say whether it reduces the damages (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Does this payment or benefit reduce the damages awarded for a person's death (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) s 26)? Insurance, superannuation, leave payments, pensions and social security, gratuities, a home bought after the death and future life premiums must not be used to reduce them.
GIVEN `the payment` IS A `A payment I received because of the death` @desc What the payment or benefit is
GIVETH A `Does it reduce my damages for the death`
`s 26 -- does this payment reduce damages for the death` `the payment` MEANS
    IF `the payment` EQUALS `something else (for example an inheritance from the estate, or workers compensation)`
    THEN `s 26 does not list it, and this Act does not say whether it reduces the damages (NOTHING-kind)`
    ELSE `no: it must not be taken into account to reduce damages for the death (the text's no, s 26)`

§§ `Part 3.2 -- Mental harm`

-- s 34 (1): no duty to take care not to cause mental harm "unless" a reasonable person in the defendant's position would
-- have foreseen that a person of normal fortitude in the plaintiff's position might suffer a recognised psychiatric
-- illness if reasonable care were not taken; (4): this does not affect the duty owed where the defendant knows, or ought
-- reasonably to know, the plaintiff is of less than normal fortitude. s 35 (1): no damages for pure mental harm from
-- negligence unless it is a recognised psychiatric illness; (2): no damages for economic loss for consequential mental
-- harm (from a bodily injury) unless it is a recognised psychiatric illness. The foreseeability is a judgment (input).
DECLARE `The mental harm I suffered` IS ONE OF
    `pure mental harm (not from a bodily injury to me)`
    `consequential mental harm (from a bodily injury to me)`

DECLARE `The damages I seek for it` IS ONE OF
    `economic loss (for example lost earnings or treatment costs)`
    `non-economic loss (for example pain and suffering)`

DECLARE `Can I get damages for my mental harm` IS ONE OF
    `the Act does not bar them: a duty of care can arise and s 35 does not stop these damages (ss 34, 35)`
    `no duty of care: a reasonable person would not have foreseen a recognised psychiatric illness in a person of normal fortitude in your position (the text's no, s 34 (1))`
    `no: damages for pure mental harm need a recognised psychiatric illness (the text's no, s 35 (1))`
    `no: damages for economic loss for consequential mental harm need a recognised psychiatric illness (the text's no, s 35 (2))`

@export Can I get damages for mental harm caused by someone's negligence (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) ss 34, 35)? There must have been a foreseeable risk of a recognised psychiatric illness to a person of normal fortitude (or the defendant knew you were more vulnerable), and pure mental harm, or economic loss from consequential mental harm, must be a recognised psychiatric illness.
GIVEN `the kind of harm` IS A `The mental harm I suffered` @desc Whether your mental harm followed a bodily injury to you (consequential) or not (pure, for example shock at seeing someone else hurt)
      `the damages sought` IS A `The damages I seek for it` @desc Whether you seek damages for economic loss or for non-economic loss
      `recognised psychiatric illness` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your mental harm is a recognised psychiatric illness (diagnosed, for example, as PTSD or a major depressive disorder)
      `foreseeable to a person of normal fortitude` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a reasonable person in the defendant's position would have foreseen that a person of normal fortitude in your position might suffer a recognised psychiatric illness if reasonable care were not taken (a judgment of the court, weighing sudden shock, whether you witnessed it, your relationships)
      `defendant knew of less than normal fortitude` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant knew, or ought reasonably to have known, that you are a person of less than normal fortitude
GIVETH A `Can I get damages for my mental harm`
`s 34, s 35 -- can I get damages for my mental harm` `the kind of harm` `the damages sought` `recognised psychiatric illness` `foreseeable to a person of normal fortitude` `defendant knew of less than normal fortitude` MEANS
    IF NOT (`foreseeable to a person of normal fortitude` OR `defendant knew of less than normal fortitude`)
    THEN `no duty of care: a reasonable person would not have foreseen a recognised psychiatric illness in a person of normal fortitude in your position (the text's no, s 34 (1))`
    ELSE IF `recognised psychiatric illness`
         THEN `the Act does not bar them: a duty of care can arise and s 35 does not stop these damages (ss 34, 35)`
         ELSE CONSIDER `the kind of harm`
              WHEN `pure mental harm (not from a bodily injury to me)` THEN `no: damages for pure mental harm need a recognised psychiatric illness (the text's no, s 35 (1))`
              WHEN `consequential mental harm (from a bodily injury to me)` THEN
                  IF `the damages sought` EQUALS `economic loss (for example lost earnings or treatment costs)`
                  THEN `no: damages for economic loss for consequential mental harm need a recognised psychiatric illness (the text's no, s 35 (2))`
                  ELSE `the Act does not bar them: a duty of care can arise and s 35 does not stop these damages (ss 34, 35)`

-- s 36 (1): liability for an injury by which someone else (A) is killed, injured or put in danger includes liability for
-- injury from mental or nervous shock received by a parent of A, a domestic partner of A, or another family member of A
-- if A was killed, injured or put in danger within the sight or hearing of that family member. s 32: "family member"
-- means a domestic partner, a parent or child, or a brother, sister, half-brother or half-sister; "parent" and "child"
-- include grandparents, step-parents, grandchildren, stepchildren and those in place of a parent. s 36 extends liability
-- ("includes"): outside it the Act says nothing (NOTHING-kind); the common law may still give a claim.
DECLARE `My relationship to the person killed, injured or endangered` IS ONE OF
    `their parent (including a step-parent, grandparent, or someone acting in place of a parent)`
    `their domestic partner, as one of the family`
    `their child (including a grandchild, stepchild, or someone they acted in place of a parent to)`
    `their brother, sister, half-brother or half-sister, as one of the family`
    `not a family member (for example a friend, a cousin, or a bystander)`

DECLARE `Does the wrongdoer's liability extend to my nervous shock` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the wrongdoer's liability includes your mental or nervous shock (s 36 (1)), subject to part 7.1 and to ss 34, 35`
    `s 36 reaches other family members only if it happened within their sight or hearing; it does not extend the liability to you, and says no more (NOTHING-kind)`
    `s 36 does not reach someone who is not a family member, and says no more (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Does the liability of the person who killed, injured or endangered my relative extend to the nervous shock I suffered (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) s 36)? It does for a parent or domestic partner, and for another family member (child, brother or sister) if it happened within their sight or hearing.
GIVEN `my relationship` IS A `My relationship to the person killed, injured or endangered` @desc How you are related to the person who was killed, injured or put in danger
      `within my sight or hearing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they were killed, injured or put in danger within your sight or hearing
GIVETH A `Does the wrongdoer's liability extend to my nervous shock`
`s 36 -- does the liability extend to my nervous shock` `my relationship` `within my sight or hearing` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my relationship`
    WHEN `their parent (including a step-parent, grandparent, or someone acting in place of a parent)` THEN `yes: the wrongdoer's liability includes your mental or nervous shock (s 36 (1)), subject to part 7.1 and to ss 34, 35`
    WHEN `their domestic partner, as one of the family` THEN `yes: the wrongdoer's liability includes your mental or nervous shock (s 36 (1)), subject to part 7.1 and to ss 34, 35`
    WHEN `not a family member (for example a friend, a cousin, or a bystander)` THEN `s 36 does not reach someone who is not a family member, and says no more (NOTHING-kind)`
    OTHERWISE
        IF `within my sight or hearing`
        THEN `yes: the wrongdoer's liability includes your mental or nervous shock (s 36 (1)), subject to part 7.1 and to ss 34, 35`
        ELSE `s 36 reaches other family members only if it happened within their sight or hearing; it does not extend the liability to you, and says no more (NOTHING-kind)`

§§ `Part 3.3 -- Motor vehicles and acts of terrorism`

-- s 37 (1): act of terrorism "means" the use or threat of action if (a) the action falls within (2) (serious violence
-- against a person, serious damage to property, endangering another's life, a serious risk to public health or safety,
-- or designed seriously to interfere with or disrupt an electronic system), (b) it is designed to influence a government
-- or intimidate the public, and (c) it is made to advance a political, religious or ideological cause. (3): an action
-- within (2) using firearms or explosives is terrorism whether or not (1) (b) is satisfied.
GIVEN `serious action` IS A BOOLEAN, `designed to influence or intimidate` IS A BOOLEAN, `for a cause` IS A BOOLEAN, `firearms or explosives` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `an act of terrorism` `serious action` `designed to influence or intimidate` `for a cause` `firearms or explosives` IF
        `serious action`
    AND `for a cause`
    AND (`designed to influence or intimidate` OR `firearms or explosives`)

-- s 38 (1): "There is no right of action for death or injury (or both) arising out of the use of a motor vehicle if the
-- death or injury (or both) are caused by an act of terrorism." (2): this does not remove the liability of someone who
-- commits or promotes the act, or of an employer to pay workers compensation. Fork F11: s 37 (3) is read as dispensing
-- with (1) (b) only, so the cause in (1) (c) is still needed.
DECLARE `Do I have a right of action for the motor vehicle injury` IS ONE OF
    `no: there is no right of action for death or injury arising out of the use of a motor vehicle caused by an act of terrorism (the text's no, s 38 (1))`
    `yes against the person who committed or promoted the act of terrorism: s 38 (1) does not remove their liability (s 38 (2) (a))`
    `s 38 does not stop it: not an injury arising out of the use of a motor vehicle caused by an act of terrorism`

@export Do I have a right of action for an injury from the use of a motor vehicle in a terrorist act (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) ss 37, 38)? There is none, except against the person who committed or promoted the act; workers compensation is not affected.
GIVEN `arising out of use of a motor vehicle` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death or injury arose out of the use of a motor vehicle
      `serious action` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the action involved serious violence against a person, serious damage to property, endangered someone else's life, created a serious risk to public health or safety, or was designed seriously to disrupt an electronic system
      `designed to influence or intimidate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the action (or threat) was designed to influence a government or to intimidate the public or a section of it
      `for a cause` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was done to advance a political, religious or ideological cause
      `firearms or explosives` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it involved the use of firearms or explosives
      `against the person who did it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are suing the person who committed or promoted the act of terrorism
GIVETH A `Do I have a right of action for the motor vehicle injury`
`s 38 -- do I have a right of action for a motor vehicle injury in a terrorist act` `arising out of use of a motor vehicle` `serious action` `designed to influence or intimidate` `for a cause` `firearms or explosives` `against the person who did it` MEANS
    IF NOT (`arising out of use of a motor vehicle` AND `an act of terrorism` `serious action` `designed to influence or intimidate` `for a cause` `firearms or explosives`)
    THEN `s 38 does not stop it: not an injury arising out of the use of a motor vehicle caused by an act of terrorism`
    ELSE IF `against the person who did it`
         THEN `yes against the person who committed or promoted the act of terrorism: s 38 (1) does not remove their liability (s 38 (2) (a))`
         ELSE `no: there is no right of action for death or injury arising out of the use of a motor vehicle caused by an act of terrorism (the text's no, s 38 (1))`

§§ `Chapter 4 -- Negligence: precautions against risk`

-- s 41: chapter 4 applies to all claims for damages for harm from negligence, however brought, but not to a claim under
-- the Workers Compensation Act 1951. s 43 (1): a person is not negligent in failing to take precautions against a risk of
-- harm "unless" (a) the risk was foreseeable, (b) it was not insignificant, and (c) a reasonable person in their position
-- would have taken them. Each is a judgment (input).
DECLARE `Was the failure to take precautions negligent` IS ONE OF
    `s 43 does not stand in the way: the risk was foreseeable and not insignificant, and a reasonable person would have taken the precautions (s 43 (1))`
    `not negligent: one of the s 43 (1) conditions is not met (the text's no)`
    `chapter 4 does not apply to a claim under the Workers Compensation Act 1951 (the text's no, s 41 (2))`

@export Was the person I blame negligent in failing to take precautions against the risk that harmed me (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) ss 41, 43)? Only if the risk was foreseeable, was not insignificant, and a reasonable person in their position would have taken those precautions.
GIVEN `workers compensation claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is a claim under the Workers Compensation Act 1951 (ACT)
      `foreseeable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the risk was foreseeable: one the person knew or ought to have known about (a judgment of the court)
      `not insignificant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the risk was not insignificant (a judgment of the court)
      `reasonable person would have taken them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a reasonable person in their position would have taken those precautions, weighing the probability and seriousness of the harm, the burden of the precautions and the social utility of the activity (a judgment of the court)
GIVETH A `Was the failure to take precautions negligent`
`s 43 -- was the failure to take precautions negligent` `workers compensation claim` `foreseeable` `not insignificant` `reasonable person would have taken them` MEANS
    IF `workers compensation claim`
    THEN `chapter 4 does not apply to a claim under the Workers Compensation Act 1951 (the text's no, s 41 (2))`
    ELSE IF `foreseeable` AND `not insignificant` AND `reasonable person would have taken them`
         THEN `s 43 does not stand in the way: the risk was foreseeable and not insignificant, and a reasonable person would have taken the precautions (s 43 (1))`
         ELSE `not negligent: one of the s 43 (1) conditions is not met (the text's no)`

§§ `Part 7.1 -- Damages for personal injuries: exclusions and limitations`

-- s 93: part 7.1 applies to all claims for damages for personal injury (including motor accident common law claims),
-- but not to claims under the Workers Compensation Act 1951. Each question below answers "part 7.1 does not apply" for a
-- workers compensation claim.

-- s 94 (1): liability for damages is excluded if the court is satisfied on the balance of probabilities that the
-- accident happened while the injured person was engaged in conduct that is an indictable offence, and that the conduct
-- contributed materially to the risk of injury. (2): the court may still award damages if satisfied the circumstances
-- are exceptional and the exclusion would operate harshly and unjustly.
DECLARE `Is my claim excluded because I was committing an offence` IS ONE OF
    `yes: liability for damages is excluded (s 94 (1))`
    `excluded, but the court may still award damages: the case is exceptional and the exclusion would operate harshly and unjustly (s 94 (2))`
    `no: s 94 excludes liability only where both s 94 (1) conditions are met (the text's no)`
    `part 7.1 does not apply to a claim under the Workers Compensation Act 1951, so s 94 does not exclude it (the text's no, s 93 (2))`

@export Is my claim for damages for personal injury excluded because I was committing an offence when I was hurt (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) s 94)? Liability is excluded if the accident happened while you were engaged in conduct that is an indictable offence that contributed materially to the risk, unless the case is exceptional and the exclusion would be harsh and unjust.
GIVEN `workers compensation claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is a claim under the Workers Compensation Act 1951 (ACT)
      `engaged in an indictable offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied, on the balance of probabilities, that the accident happened while you were engaged in conduct that is an indictable offence
      `contributed materially to the risk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied, on the balance of probabilities, that your conduct contributed materially to the risk of injury
      `exceptional, harsh and unjust` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied that the circumstances are exceptional and that the exclusion would operate harshly and unjustly
GIVETH A `Is my claim excluded because I was committing an offence`
`s 94 -- is my claim excluded because I was committing an offence` `workers compensation claim` `engaged in an indictable offence` `contributed materially to the risk` `exceptional, harsh and unjust` MEANS
    IF `workers compensation claim`
    THEN `part 7.1 does not apply to a claim under the Workers Compensation Act 1951, so s 94 does not exclude it (the text's no, s 93 (2))`
    ELSE IF NOT (`engaged in an indictable offence` AND `contributed materially to the risk`)
         THEN `no: s 94 excludes liability only where both s 94 (1) conditions are met (the text's no)`
         ELSE IF `exceptional, harsh and unjust`
              THEN `excluded, but the court may still award damages: the case is exceptional and the exclusion would operate harshly and unjustly (s 94 (2))`
              ELSE `yes: liability for damages is excluded (s 94 (1))`

-- ss 95-97: "contributory negligence must be presumed if" ...; each can be rebutted "only" as the section says; if not
-- rebutted, the damages must be reduced to the extent the court considers just and equitable. The "if" failing is the
-- text's no to the presumption; the court's own finding of contributory negligence on the evidence is untouched (s 102).
DECLARE `Is contributory negligence presumed against me` IS ONE OF
    `yes: contributory negligence is presumed, and your damages must be reduced as far as the court considers just and equitable`
    `presumed, but rebutted: you have shown what the section allows, so no presumption stands`
    `not presumed under this section (the text's no): the defendant must prove any contributory negligence in the ordinary way`
    `part 7.1 does not apply to a claim under the Workers Compensation Act 1951 (the text's no, s 93 (2))`

-- s 95: the injured person was at least 16 and intoxicated at the accident, and the defendant claims contributory
-- negligence. Rebutted only if the intoxication did not contribute to the accident, or was not self-induced. s 92:
-- "intoxicated" -- under the influence of alcohol or a drug so that capacity to exercise appropriate care and skill is
-- impaired (input).
@export Is contributory negligence presumed against me because I was intoxicated when I was injured (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) s 95)? Yes if you were at least 16 and intoxicated and the defendant claims it, unless you prove the intoxication did not contribute to the accident or was not self-induced.
GIVEN `workers compensation claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is a claim under the Workers Compensation Act 1951 (ACT)
      `age at the accident` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age, in whole years, at the time of the accident
      `intoxicated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were intoxicated at the time: under the influence of alcohol or a drug so that your capacity to exercise appropriate care and skill was impaired
      `defendant claims contributory negligence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant claims contributory negligence against you
      `did not contribute` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you can prove, on the balance of probabilities, that the intoxication did not contribute to the accident
      `not self-induced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you can prove, on the balance of probabilities, that the intoxication was not self-induced (for example, your drink was spiked)
GIVETH A `Is contributory negligence presumed against me`
`s 95 -- is contributory negligence presumed because I was intoxicated` `workers compensation claim` `age at the accident` `intoxicated` `defendant claims contributory negligence` `did not contribute` `not self-induced` MEANS
    IF `workers compensation claim`
    THEN `part 7.1 does not apply to a claim under the Workers Compensation Act 1951 (the text's no, s 93 (2))`
    ELSE IF NOT (`age at the accident` AT LEAST 16 AND `intoxicated` AND `defendant claims contributory negligence`)
         THEN `not presumed under this section (the text's no): the defendant must prove any contributory negligence in the ordinary way`
         ELSE IF `did not contribute` OR `not self-induced`
              THEN `presumed, but rebutted: you have shown what the section allows, so no presumption stands`
              ELSE `yes: contributory negligence is presumed, and your damages must be reduced as far as the court considers just and equitable`

-- s 96: the injured person was at least 16, relied on the care and skill of a person (A) who was intoxicated at the
-- accident, knew or ought to have known it, the accident was caused by A's negligence, and the defendant claims
-- contributory negligence. A passenger in a motor vehicle is taken to rely on the driver (4). Rebutted only if the
-- intoxication did not contribute to the accident, or the injured person could not reasonably be expected to have
-- avoided the risk.
@export Is contributory negligence presumed against me because I relied on someone who was intoxicated, such as a drunk driver (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) s 96)? Yes if you were at least 16, knew or ought to have known they were intoxicated, their negligence caused the accident and the defendant claims it, unless you prove the intoxication did not contribute or you could not reasonably have avoided the risk.
GIVEN `workers compensation claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is a claim under the Workers Compensation Act 1951 (ACT)
      `age at the accident` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age, in whole years, at the time of the accident
      `relied on an intoxicated person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you relied on the care and skill of a person who was intoxicated at the time (as a passenger in a motor vehicle you are taken to rely on the driver)
      `knew or ought to have known` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you knew, or ought to have known, that they were intoxicated
      `caused by their negligence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the accident was caused by that person's negligence
      `defendant claims contributory negligence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant claims contributory negligence against you
      `did not contribute` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you can prove, on the balance of probabilities, that the intoxication did not contribute to the accident
      `could not reasonably have avoided the risk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you can prove, on the balance of probabilities, that you could not reasonably be expected to have avoided the risk
GIVETH A `Is contributory negligence presumed against me`
`s 96 -- is contributory negligence presumed because I relied on an intoxicated person` `workers compensation claim` `age at the accident` `relied on an intoxicated person` `knew or ought to have known` `caused by their negligence` `defendant claims contributory negligence` `did not contribute` `could not reasonably have avoided the risk` MEANS
    IF `workers compensation claim`
    THEN `part 7.1 does not apply to a claim under the Workers Compensation Act 1951 (the text's no, s 93 (2))`
    ELSE IF NOT (`age at the accident` AT LEAST 16 AND `relied on an intoxicated person` AND `knew or ought to have known` AND `caused by their negligence` AND `defendant claims contributory negligence`)
         THEN `not presumed under this section (the text's no): the defendant must prove any contributory negligence in the ordinary way`
         ELSE IF `did not contribute` OR `could not reasonably have avoided the risk`
              THEN `presumed, but rebutted: you have shown what the section allows, so no presumption stands`
              ELSE `yes: contributory negligence is presumed, and your damages must be reduced as far as the court considers just and equitable`

-- s 97 (1): the injured person was injured in a motor accident, was at least 16, and (a) was not wearing a seatbelt as
-- required under the Road Transport (Safety and Traffic Management) Act 1999, or (b) one of these contributed to the
-- accident or the extent of the injury: (i) not wearing a helmet as required; (ii) being a passenger in or on a vehicle
-- with a passenger compartment but not in it. Rebuttal "only" as (3) and (4) provide: for (a), the injury was less
-- serious than it would have been with a seatbelt, or the person could not fasten one without help; for (b) (ii), the
-- person could not reasonably be expected to have avoided the risk. No rebuttal is provided for the helmet (finding 7).
-- s 97 does not require the defendant to claim contributory negligence (finding 8).
@export Is contributory negligence presumed against me for a motor accident injury because I was not wearing a seatbelt or helmet, or was outside the passenger compartment (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) s 97)? Yes if you were at least 16, with rebuttals only for the seatbelt and the compartment.
GIVEN `workers compensation claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is a claim under the Workers Compensation Act 1951 (ACT)
      `motor accident` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were injured in a motor accident (one caused by, or arising out of the use of, a motor vehicle)
      `age at the accident` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age, in whole years, at the time of the accident
      `no seatbelt as required` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were not wearing a seatbelt at the time as the road transport law required
      `no helmet as required, and it contributed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were not wearing a helmet as the road transport law required, and that contributed to the accident or the extent of your injury
      `outside the passenger compartment, and it contributed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were a passenger in or on a vehicle with a passenger compartment but not in it, and that contributed to the accident or the extent of your injury
      `injury less serious than with a seatbelt` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you can prove that your injury was less serious than it would have been had you been wearing a seatbelt
      `could not fasten a seatbelt unaided` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you can prove that you were not capable of fastening a seatbelt without someone else's help
      `could not reasonably have avoided the risk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you can prove that you could not reasonably be expected to have avoided the risk of being outside the passenger compartment
GIVETH A `Is contributory negligence presumed against me`
`s 97 -- is contributory negligence presumed for no seatbelt or helmet` `workers compensation claim` `motor accident` `age at the accident` `no seatbelt as required` `no helmet as required, and it contributed` `outside the passenger compartment, and it contributed` `injury less serious than with a seatbelt` `could not fasten a seatbelt unaided` `could not reasonably have avoided the risk` MEANS
    IF `workers compensation claim`
    THEN `part 7.1 does not apply to a claim under the Workers Compensation Act 1951 (the text's no, s 93 (2))`
    ELSE IF NOT (`motor accident` AND `age at the accident` AT LEAST 16 AND (`no seatbelt as required` OR `no helmet as required, and it contributed` OR `outside the passenger compartment, and it contributed`))
         THEN `not presumed under this section (the text's no): the defendant must prove any contributory negligence in the ordinary way`
         ELSE IF `no helmet as required, and it contributed` OR `the seatbelt ground stands` OR `the compartment ground stands`
              THEN `yes: contributory negligence is presumed, and your damages must be reduced as far as the court considers just and equitable`
              ELSE `presumed, but rebutted: you have shown what the section allows, so no presumption stands`
    WHERE
        `the seatbelt ground stands` MEANS `no seatbelt as required` AND NOT (`injury less serious than with a seatbelt` OR `could not fasten a seatbelt unaided`)
        `the compartment ground stands` MEANS `outside the passenger compartment, and it contributed` AND NOT `could not reasonably have avoided the risk`

-- s 98: in assessing damages for loss of earnings (past and future), the court must disregard earnings above 3 times
-- average weekly earnings a week. AWE is the ABS figure for the ACT (all males total earnings, seasonally adjusted):
-- an input, as the publication is not deposited.
@export How much of my weekly earnings can count in damages for loss of earnings (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) s 98)? Earnings above 3 times average weekly earnings a week are disregarded. The answer is dollars a week.
GIVEN `weekly earnings` IS A NUMBER @desc Your earnings (or earning capacity) a week that the loss of earnings claim is about, in dollars
      `average weekly earnings` IS A NUMBER @desc Average weekly earnings: the ABS figure for the ACT, all males total earnings, seasonally adjusted, in dollars a week
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 98 -- how much of my weekly earnings counts` `weekly earnings` `average weekly earnings` MEANS
    IF `weekly earnings` GREATER THAN `the limit`
    THEN `the limit`
    ELSE `weekly earnings`
    WHERE
        `the limit` MEANS 3 TIMES `average weekly earnings`

§§ `Part 7.2 -- Loss of capacity to perform domestic services`

-- s 100 (1): a person liable for an injury caused by a wrong is liable for damages for any resulting impairment or loss
-- of the injured person's capacity to perform domestic services they might reasonably have been expected to perform for
-- their household. (2): it does not matter whether the services were for others or only themselves, unpaid, would not
-- have to be paid for, or are being done by others. s 16 (3) (c): not for the estate where the wrong caused the death.
DECLARE `Can I claim for losing my capacity to do domestic services` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the liability includes damages for that loss, whoever did or does the services, and whether or not anyone is paid (s 100)`
    `s 100 covers only services you might reasonably have been expected to perform for your household; it says nothing more (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Can I claim damages for losing the capacity to do housework and other domestic services because of my injury (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) s 100)? Yes, for services you might reasonably have been expected to do for your household, even if unpaid, only for yourself, or now done by others.
GIVEN `expected to perform for my household` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, had you not been injured, you might reasonably have been expected to perform those domestic services for your household (for yourself or others)
GIVETH A `Can I claim for losing my capacity to do domestic services`
`s 100 -- can I claim for losing capacity to do domestic services` `expected to perform for my household` MEANS
    IF `expected to perform for my household`
    THEN `yes: the liability includes damages for that loss, whoever did or does the services, and whether or not anyone is paid (s 100)`
    ELSE `s 100 covers only services you might reasonably have been expected to perform for your household; it says nothing more (NOTHING-kind)`

§§ `Part 7.3 -- Contributory negligence: how much are my damages reduced`

-- s 102 (1): damages are reduced to the extent the court considers just and equitable having regard to the claimant's
-- share in the responsibility; s 47 lets the court reduce by 100%. (2): not reduced for personal injury where the wrong
-- was a breach of statutory duty. (3): an Act or contract limiting liability caps the amount. (5): subject to part 7.1.
-- s 27: damages for a death under part 3.1 are not reduced for the dead person's contributory negligence, subject to
-- part 7.1. s 168 (3): s 102 (2) does not apply to a claim against an occupier. Fork F12: "subject to part 7.1" is read
-- as letting an unrebutted s 95-97 presumption reduce damages even where s 27 or s 102 (2) would not.
@export How much are my damages after the reduction for my own contributory negligence (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) ss 27, 47, 102)? They are cut by the share of responsibility the court puts on you (up to 100%), but not for a personal injury from a breach of statutory duty, or in a claim for a death, unless a part 7.1 presumption stands. The answer is dollars.
GIVEN `damages before the reduction` IS A NUMBER @desc The damages, in dollars, before any reduction for your contributory negligence
      `my share` IS A NUMBER @desc The percentage of responsibility the court considers just and equitable to put on you (0 to 100; a judgment of the court)
      `personal injury from a breach of statutory duty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you suffered personal injury and the wrong was a breach of statutory duty
      `a claim against an occupier of premises` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the claim is against the occupier of premises for injury or damage from the state of the premises (s 168)
      `a claim for a death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether this is a claim under part 3.1 for the death of a person who was partly at fault
      `a part 7.1 presumption stands` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether contributory negligence is presumed under s 95, 96 or 97 and the presumption has not been rebutted
      `a limit applies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an Act or contract limiting liability applies to the claim
      `the limit` IS A NUMBER @desc If so, the maximum limit, in dollars (otherwise any number; it is not used)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 102 -- my damages after contributory negligence` `damages before the reduction` `my share` `personal injury from a breach of statutory duty` `a claim against an occupier of premises` `a claim for a death` `a part 7.1 presumption stands` `a limit applies` `the limit` MEANS
    IF `a limit applies` AND `the reduced damages` GREATER THAN `the limit`
    THEN `the limit`
    ELSE `the reduced damages`
    WHERE
        `no reduction allowed` MEANS
            (   `a claim for a death`
             OR (`personal injury from a breach of statutory duty` AND NOT `a claim against an occupier of premises`))
            AND NOT `a part 7.1 presumption stands`
        `the share used` MEANS
            IF `my share` GREATER THAN 100
            THEN 100
            ELSE IF `my share` LESS THAN 0
                 THEN 0
                 ELSE `my share`
        `the reduced damages` MEANS
            IF `no reduction allowed`
            THEN `damages before the reduction`
            ELSE `damages before the reduction` TIMES (100 MINUS `the share used`) DIVIDED BY 100

§§ `Chapter 7A -- Proportionate liability`

-- s 107B (2): an apportionable claim is a claim for economic loss or damage to property arising from a failure to take
-- reasonable care, or under the Australian Consumer Law (ACT) s 236 for a contravention of its part 3.1. (3), (4): "none
-- of the following is an apportionable claim": a claim arising out of personal injury; a consumer claim; a claim under
-- the Discrimination Act 1991; a motor accident common law claim; a workers compensation claim. s 107F: on an
-- apportionable claim each concurrent wrongdoer is liable only for its just share. A consumer claim (s 107C) is asked.
DECLARE `What my claim is for` IS ONE OF
    `personal injury (or loss arising out of it)`
    `economic loss or damage to property, from a failure to take reasonable care`
    `economic loss or damage to property, under the Australian Consumer Law (ACT) s 236 for a contravention of its part 3.1 (unfair practices)`
    `something else`

DECLARE `Is my claim apportionable` IS ONE OF
    `yes: an apportionable claim; each wrongdoer is liable only for the share the court considers just (ss 107B, 107F)`
    `no: a claim arising out of personal injury is not an apportionable claim (the text's no, s 107B (3) (a)); chapter 7A does not limit any wrongdoer's liability`
    `no: a consumer claim is not an apportionable claim (the text's no, s 107B (3) (b))`
    `no: Discrimination Act, motor accident common law and workers compensation claims are not apportionable claims (the text's no, s 107B (4))`
    `chapter 7A applies only to the claims s 107B (2) lists; this is not one, and it says nothing more (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Is my claim an apportionable claim, so that each of several wrongdoers pays only its own share (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) ch 7A, ss 107B, 107C)? Claims arising out of personal injury, consumer claims, discrimination, motor accident and workers compensation claims are not.
GIVEN `what it is for` IS A `What my claim is for` @desc What your claim is for
      `a consumer claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is a consumer claim: by you as an individual, about goods or services you acquired for personal, domestic or household use (and did not hold yourself out as buying to resupply, manufacture or repair), or personal financial advice given to you
      `discrimination, motor accident or workers compensation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is a claim under the Discrimination Act 1991, a motor accident common law claim (Motor Accident Injuries Act 2019 ch 5) or a workers compensation claim
GIVETH A `Is my claim apportionable`
`ch 7A -- is my claim apportionable` `what it is for` `a consumer claim` `discrimination, motor accident or workers compensation` MEANS
    IF `what it is for` EQUALS `personal injury (or loss arising out of it)`
    THEN `no: a claim arising out of personal injury is not an apportionable claim (the text's no, s 107B (3) (a)); chapter 7A does not limit any wrongdoer's liability`
    ELSE IF `discrimination, motor accident or workers compensation`
         THEN `no: Discrimination Act, motor accident common law and workers compensation claims are not apportionable claims (the text's no, s 107B (4))`
         ELSE IF `a consumer claim`
              THEN `no: a consumer claim is not an apportionable claim (the text's no, s 107B (3) (b))`
              ELSE IF `what it is for` EQUALS `something else`
                   THEN `chapter 7A applies only to the claims s 107B (2) lists; this is not one, and it says nothing more (NOTHING-kind)`
                   ELSE `yes: an apportionable claim; each wrongdoer is liable only for the share the court considers just (ss 107B, 107F)`

§§ `Chapter 8 -- Public and other authorities: roads`

-- s 108: chapter 8 applies to civil liability in tort (however sued for), but not to motor accident common law claims or
-- workers compensation claims. s 113 (1): a public or other authority "is not liable" for harm from a failure to
-- maintain, repair or renew a road (or to consider doing so) "unless" at the time it knew, or ought reasonably to have
-- known, of the particular risk that materialised. "Public or other authority" (s 109) is asked: the Territory, an
-- administrative unit, a territory authority, or a prescribed entity or function.
DECLARE `Can the authority be liable for not repairing the road` IS ONE OF
    `s 113 does not bar the claim: the authority knew, or ought reasonably to have known, of the particular risk (s 113 (1)); whether it owed and breached a duty is decided as usual (s 113 (2))`
    `no: the authority is not liable for the failure unless it knew or ought reasonably to have known of the particular risk (the text's no, s 113 (1))`
    `chapter 8 does not apply to motor accident common law claims or workers compensation claims (the text's no, s 108 (3))`
    `s 113 is about a public or other authority's failure to maintain, repair or renew a road; it says nothing about this (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Can a public authority be liable to me for harm caused by its failure to maintain or repair a road or footpath (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) ss 108, 113)? Not unless, at the time, it knew or ought reasonably to have known of the particular risk that caused the harm.
GIVEN `motor accident or workers compensation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is a motor accident common law claim (Motor Accident Injuries Act 2019 ch 5) or a workers compensation claim
      `a public or other authority` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant is the Territory, an ACT government administrative unit, a territory authority, or an entity prescribed by regulation
      `failure to maintain a road` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the harm arose from its failure to maintain, repair or renew (or consider maintaining, repairing or renewing) a street, road, lane, cyclepath, footpath or paved area open to or used by the public
      `knew or ought to have known the risk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, at the time of the failure, the authority knew, or ought reasonably to have known, of the particular risk that caused your harm
GIVETH A `Can the authority be liable for not repairing the road`
`s 113 -- can the authority be liable for not repairing the road` `motor accident or workers compensation` `a public or other authority` `failure to maintain a road` `knew or ought to have known the risk` MEANS
    IF `motor accident or workers compensation`
    THEN `chapter 8 does not apply to motor accident common law claims or workers compensation claims (the text's no, s 108 (3))`
    ELSE IF NOT (`a public or other authority` AND `failure to maintain a road`)
         THEN `s 113 is about a public or other authority's failure to maintain, repair or renew a road; it says nothing about this (NOTHING-kind)`
         ELSE IF `knew or ought to have known the risk`
              THEN `s 113 does not bar the claim: the authority knew, or ought reasonably to have known, of the particular risk (s 113 (1)); whether it owed and breached a duty is decided as usual (s 113 (2))`
              ELSE `no: the authority is not liable for the failure unless it knew or ought reasonably to have known of the particular risk (the text's no, s 113 (1))`

§§ `Chapter 8A -- Institutional child abuse`

-- s 114BBD: an organisation responsible for a child (it exercises care, supervision or authority over the child, s
-- 114BBA) must take reasonable precautions to prevent an individual associated with it from perpetrating child abuse
-- against the child in connection with that responsibility. (3): if such an individual does, the organisation is taken
-- to have breached the duty unless it establishes that it took reasonable precautions. s 114BBB: division 8A.1A.2
-- applies to child abuse that happens on or after the day it commenced: 13 November 2025 (A2025-32, endnote 3).
-- s 114BBC: associated individuals include office holders, officers, owners, employees, agents, volunteers and
-- contractors, and for a religious organisation its religious leaders and personnel (asked as a fact).
-- s 114AA: child abuse means physical or sexual abuse of a child.
`the day division 8A.1A.2 commenced` MEANS `the calendar date` 13 11 2025

DECLARE `Is the organisation taken to have breached its duty` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the organisation is taken to have breached its duty of care to prevent the child abuse (s 114BBD (3))`
    `no: the organisation established that it took reasonable precautions to prevent the abuse (the text's no, s 114BBD (3))`
    `no: division 8A.1A.2 applies only to child abuse on or after 13 November 2025 (the text's no, s 114BBB)`
    `s 114BBD (3) applies only where the organisation was responsible for the child and the abuser was associated with it and acted in connection with that responsibility; it says nothing here (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Is the organisation taken to have breached its duty to prevent the child abuse I suffered (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) ss 114BBA-114BBD)? For abuse on or after 13 November 2025, an organisation responsible for a child is taken to have breached its duty if someone associated with it abused the child in connection with that responsibility, unless it proves it took reasonable precautions.
GIVEN `the abuse happened on` IS A DATE @desc The day the child abuse happened (or is alleged to have happened); if it went on over a period, its first day
      `organisation responsible for me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the organisation (or a part of it, or another organisation it delegated to) exercised care, supervision or authority over you as a child
      `abuser associated with it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the abuser was associated with the organisation: an office holder, officer, owner, employee, agent, volunteer or contractor, or for a religious organisation a religious leader or other member of its personnel
      `in connection with its responsibility` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the abuse was perpetrated in connection with the organisation's responsibility for you
      `organisation proves reasonable precautions` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the organisation establishes that it took reasonable precautions to prevent the abuse (a judgment of the court)
GIVETH A `Is the organisation taken to have breached its duty`
`s 114BBD -- is the organisation taken to have breached its duty` `the abuse happened on` `organisation responsible for me` `abuser associated with it` `in connection with its responsibility` `organisation proves reasonable precautions` MEANS
    IF DATE_SERIAL `the abuse happened on` LESS THAN DATE_SERIAL `the day division 8A.1A.2 commenced`
    THEN `no: division 8A.1A.2 applies only to child abuse on or after 13 November 2025 (the text's no, s 114BBB)`
    ELSE IF NOT (`organisation responsible for me` AND `abuser associated with it` AND `in connection with its responsibility`)
         THEN `s 114BBD (3) applies only where the organisation was responsible for the child and the abuser was associated with it and acted in connection with that responsibility; it says nothing here (NOTHING-kind)`
         ELSE IF `organisation proves reasonable precautions`
              THEN `no: the organisation established that it took reasonable precautions to prevent the abuse (the text's no, s 114BBD (3))`
              ELSE `yes: the organisation is taken to have breached its duty of care to prevent the child abuse (s 114BBD (3))`

-- s 114BE (1): an organisation responsible for a child is vicariously liable for child abuse perpetrated against the
-- child by its employee if the apparent performance of a role in which the organisation placed the employee supplies the
-- occasion, and the employee takes advantage of it. s 114BC: employee includes an individual akin to an employee
-- (carrying out activities that are part of the organisation's ordinary activities and for its benefit). (3): the common
-- law of vicarious liability is untouched, so outside s 114BE the answer is NOTHING-kind. s 114BA: whenever the abuse
-- happened.
DECLARE `Is the organisation vicariously liable` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the organisation is vicariously liable for the child abuse its employee perpetrated (s 114BE (1)), whenever it happened (s 114BA)`
    `s 114BE does not make it liable on these facts; the common law of vicarious liability still applies (s 114BE (3)) (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Is the organisation vicariously liable for child abuse its employee committed against me (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) ss 114BC, 114BE)? Yes, whenever the abuse happened, if it was responsible for you, the abuser was its employee (or akin to one), the role it gave them supplied the occasion and they took advantage of it.
GIVEN `organisation responsible for me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the organisation exercised care, supervision or authority over you as a child
      `employee or akin` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the abuser was its employee, or akin to one: carrying out activities that were part of the organisation's ordinary activities and for its benefit (for example a priest, a volunteer coach)
      `role supplied the occasion` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the apparent performance of the role in which the organisation placed the abuser supplied the occasion for the abuse (a judgment of the court, weighing their authority, power or control over you, your trust, and their ability to achieve intimacy with you)
      `took advantage` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the abuser took advantage of that occasion to abuse you
GIVETH A `Is the organisation vicariously liable`
`s 114BE -- is the organisation vicariously liable` `organisation responsible for me` `employee or akin` `role supplied the occasion` `took advantage` MEANS
    IF `organisation responsible for me` AND `employee or akin` AND `role supplied the occasion` AND `took advantage`
    THEN `yes: the organisation is vicariously liable for the child abuse its employee perpetrated (s 114BE (1)), whenever it happened (s 114BA)`
    ELSE `s 114BE does not make it liable on these facts; the common law of vicarious liability still applies (s 114BE (3)) (NOTHING-kind)`

-- s 114D: an unincorporated body against which a child abuse claim is brought may, with the nominee's consent, nominate
-- an entity capable of being sued as defendant; once a proceeding has started, no later than 120 days after the day it
-- started, unless the court orders otherwise. s 114E (1): the plaintiff may apply for a related trust to be appointed as
-- defendant if the body has not nominated within 120 days after the proceeding started, or the nominee is not capable of
-- being sued or lacks sufficient assets. The body's 120 days is a regulative rule (MAY: not nominating is no breach; when
-- the period passes unused the plaintiff's application opens).
DECLARE `Unincorporated body party` IS ONE OF `the unincorporated body`
DECLARE `Unincorporated body step` IS ONE OF `nominate a defendant`

GIVEN `the 120th day serial` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Unincorporated body party` `Unincorporated body step`
`the unincorporated body may nominate a defendant` `the 120th day serial` MEANS
    PARTY `the unincorporated body`
    MAY `nominate a defendant`
    WITHIN `the 120th day serial`

DECLARE `Can I ask for a related trust to be appointed defendant` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the body did not nominate a defendant within 120 days after the proceeding started (s 114E (1) (a))`
    `yes: the entity the body nominated is not capable of being sued, or lacks the assets to meet a judgment (s 114E (1) (b))`
    `not yet: the body has until the 120th day after the proceeding started to nominate a defendant (s 114D (3))`
    `no: the body nominated a defendant that can be sued and can meet a judgment; the claim goes against the nominee (the text's no, s 114D (4), s 114E (1))`

@export Can I ask the court to appoint a related trust as defendant for the unincorporated body (such as a church or club) I am suing for child abuse (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) ss 114D, 114E)? Yes, if the body has not nominated a defendant within 120 days after the proceeding started, or its nominee cannot be sued or lacks the assets to pay.
GIVEN `proceeding started on` IS A DATE @desc The day your proceeding for the child abuse claim against the unincorporated body started
      `body nominated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the body has nominated an entity (with its consent) to act as defendant
      `nominated on` IS A DATE @desc If so, the day it did (otherwise any date; it is not used)
      `nominee can be sued` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If so, whether the nominee is capable of being sued
      `nominee has sufficient assets` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If so, whether the nominee has sufficient assets or property to meet any judgment or order on the claim
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `Can I ask for a related trust to be appointed defendant`
`s 114E -- can I ask for a related trust to be appointed defendant` `proceeding started on` `body nominated` `nominated on` `nominee can be sued` `nominee has sufficient assets` `today` MEANS
    IF `body nominated` AND `the day` `nominated on` `is on or before` `the 120th day`
    THEN IF `nominee can be sued` AND `nominee has sufficient assets`
         THEN `no: the body nominated a defendant that can be sued and can meet a judgment; the claim goes against the nominee (the text's no, s 114D (4), s 114E (1))`
         ELSE `yes: the entity the body nominated is not capable of being sued, or lacks the assets to meet a judgment (s 114E (1) (b))`
    ELSE CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`the unincorporated body may nominate a defendant` (DATE_SERIAL `the 120th day`)) 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL (DATE_SERIAL `the day looked at`)))
         WHEN FULFILLED THEN `yes: the body did not nominate a defendant within 120 days after the proceeding started (s 114E (1) (a))`
         OTHERWISE `not yet: the body has until the 120th day after the proceeding started to nominate a defendant (s 114D (3))`
    WHERE
        `the 120th day` MEANS `the date` 120 `days after` `proceeding started on`
        `the day looked at` MEANS
            IF `body nominated`
            THEN `nominated on`
            ELSE `today`

§§ `Chapter 14 -- The most my lawyer can be paid`

-- s 181: if the amount recovered on a claim for personal injury damages is $50,000 or less, a lawyer is not entitled to
-- be paid more for legal services to the plaintiff than the greater of 20% of the amount recovered and $10,000 (the
-- relevant percentage and amount, unless a regulation prescribes others: not deposited). The amount recovered includes
-- a settlement, but not costs or interest; costs do not include disbursements. Subject to s 182 (indemnity costs after
-- an offer of compromise), s 183 (costs excluded by the court) and s 184 (additional costs the court allows): the
-- additional costs allowed are an input. Above $50,000, s 181 does not apply: NOTHING (no s 181 cap).
@export What is the most my lawyer can be paid for legal services on my personal injury damages claim (Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (ACT) s 181)? If you recover $50,000 or less, the greater of 20% of what you recover and $10,000, plus any additional costs the court allows; no cap under s 181 above $50,000. The answer is dollars, excluding disbursements.
GIVEN `amount recovered` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount you recovered on the claim, in dollars, including under a settlement, but not counting costs or interest
      `additional costs allowed` IS A NUMBER @desc Any additional costs a court or taxing officer has ordered your lawyer is entitled to because of the claim's complexity or a party's behaviour (s 184), in dollars (0 if none)
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 181 -- the most my lawyer can be paid` `amount recovered` `additional costs allowed` MEANS
    IF `amount recovered` GREATER THAN 50000
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE JUST (`the maximum` PLUS `additional costs allowed`)
    WHERE
        `the percentage part` MEANS `amount recovered` TIMES 20 DIVIDED BY 100
        `the maximum` MEANS
            IF `the percentage part` GREATER THAN 10000
            THEN `the percentage part`
            ELSE 10000
